# German-learning deck rules

This workspace is an evolving German-learning system. Treat these conventions
as the current rules, and update this file when the user adds or changes a
rule. Do not infer a permanent rule from a one-off request without recording
the user's confirmation here.

## Folder structure

- `ANKI/` contains only cumulative decks and their supporting builders/indexes.
  These are kept long term and incremented as new lessons are processed.
- Each DW lesson has its own numbered folder under its unit, for example
  `DW Deutsch lernen/A1/<unit>/<lesson>/`. Keep that lesson's Markdown, its
  lesson-specific deck, and a `Materials/` subfolder together there. Download
  the official script/vocabulary PDF for every lesson into `Materials/`; keep
  other verified official lesson PDFs there too. These are temporary,
  self-contained learning materials.
- Keep the vocabulary captured from web or lesson Markdown in
  `ANKI/lesson-vocabulary.json`, with the lesson ID, title, source URL, source
  Markdown path, and German/English entries. This makes the cumulative decks
  reproducible and lets the scan indexes show their source.

## Deck types

### Cumulative decks

- Keep one cumulative Nico’s Weg A1 review system, divided into the three
  decks below by the retrieval skill—not a single undifferentiated giant deck.
  A later course may join these cumulative decks only when the learner chooses
  to add it.
- Internal cumulative deck names begin with `DW A1 —` (Vocabulary, Articles,
  and Sentences). This label remains reserved for Nico’s Weg A1 until the
  learner chooses to merge another course.
- `ANKI/articles.apkg`: one note per noun. Its cards practise `der`, `die`, or
  `das` by typed recall only. Do not add multiple-choice buttons. Keep the noun
  alone on the front so the plural cannot leak the answer; after answering,
  show the article with the noun, the source plural whenever one is supplied,
  and a gender-pattern hint only when the pattern is dependable.
- `ANKI/vocabulary.apkg`: general vocabulary. Add new lesson vocabulary without
  removing still-valid existing cards. Front: a precise English meaning or
  constrained context; answer: one typed German lexical item. For nouns, type
  the noun without its article so this deck tests lexical recall rather than
  duplicating the Articles deck; show the complete source form and plural on
  the back. For verbs, type the infinitive without placeholders such as
  `(etwas)`, then show complements and supplied forms on the back. Keep source
  glosses, form notes, and lesson provenance on the back. Merge exact repeats
  across lessons instead of creating or silently overwriting duplicate cards.
- `ANKI/sentences.apkg`: a selective cumulative sentence-practice deck. Add
  only durable, source-based sentences or short patterns that practise a
  distinct, reusable skill. Use a small manually curated set per lesson, not a
  mechanical conversion of transcript lines. Prefer tightly cued German
  production, useful micro-dialogue responses, contextual completion, and
  contextual verb forms. A card must have one unambiguous target, corrective
  feedback, and a reason to exist beyond a Vocabulary or Articles card. Do not
  generate German-to-English mirrors, multiple-choice variants, or one card per
  dictionary form. Do not test a tense merely because a glossary lists its
  principal parts; practise forms that the lesson actually makes useful.
  This remains the home for verbs and grammar: do not create a broad separate
  verb or grammar deck unless the learner later asks for one. Keep its curated
  source records and scan index current.
- Across cumulative decks, quality and retrieval purpose outrank card count.
  Do not make two cards that test substantially the same recall. Fixed
  expressions may live in Vocabulary; productive sentence patterns, grammar,
  and responses belong in Sentences; noun gender belongs in Articles.
- Do not add recorded audio, synthetic audio, text-to-speech, sound fields, or
  audio-only cards to the cumulative Articles, Vocabulary, or Sentences decks,
  or to lesson Vocabulary decks. A separately requested pronunciation resource
  is outside this rule; it must not silently feed audio into these review decks.
  Audio may return to these decks only if the learner explicitly changes this
  rule later.
- Preserve existing notes, card IDs, and scheduling data whenever rebuilding a
  cumulative deck. Never recreate one from scratch unless the user explicitly
  asks to reset it.
- Maintain the relevant scan index whenever a cumulative deck changes.

### Lesson-specific decks

- Create one in its lesson folder alongside the Markdown file, using the name
  `<Lesson>_Vocabulary.apkg`.
- The internal Anki deck name is `DW — <Lesson> Vocabulary (typed)`.
- Use a typed production design: an English meaning or constrained context on
  the front, typed German, then the correct German and source notes on the
  back.
- Include the lesson's lexical vocabulary and useful fixed expressions. Do not
  duplicate an item that has deliberately been assigned to the cumulative
  Sentences deck as a grammar or productive-pattern target. Add its nouns to
  the cumulative Articles deck and its vocabulary entries to the cumulative
  Vocabulary deck. Treat these lesson decks as a typed introduction to the
  cumulative system, rather than a separate long-term review queue.

## When processing a lesson

1. Read the lesson Markdown and its vocabulary page/source if available.
2. Capture the vocabulary in `ANKI/lesson-vocabulary.json`; avoid duplicate
   entries and preserve useful plural forms.
3. Build/update the lesson deck beside the Markdown file.
4. Increment `ANKI/articles.apkg`, `ANKI/vocabulary.apkg`, and, where the
   lesson contains useful reusable language, `ANKI/sentences.apkg`, keeping
   their existing progress and indexes intact.
5. Validate every produced `.apkg` for SQLite integrity, linked note/card
   counts, empty media, and absence of audio/TTS before delivering it.

## Card-quality gate

Before adding a card, confirm all of the following:

1. The target is source-backed, useful beyond one scene, and appropriate to the
   learner's current level.
2. The prompt makes one answer reasonably clear; add a register, grammatical,
   or situational cue when English alone is ambiguous.
3. The card tests active recall rather than recognition or guessing.
4. The answer is the smallest natural unit that fulfils the learning goal.
5. No existing card already tests the same knowledge in substantially the same
   way.
6. The back gives concise corrective feedback: canonical answer first, then
   only a useful form, plural, contrast, or source note.

When a lesson is processed, curate sentence cards explicitly. As a working
limit rather than a quota, most short lessons should contribute roughly two to
five sentence/grammar cards; adding none is better than adding weak cards.

## Development status

The deck system is actively being developed. Prefer the current documented
rules over assumptions; ask before introducing a new enduring study rule when
the user's intent is unclear. Record confirmed rule changes in this file and
adapt the builders so they are applied in future sessions.

For Anki *implementation* choices only (card templates, question/answer
formats, media, or interaction), consult
[`ANKI/anki-card-design-reference.md`](ANKI/anki-card-design-reference.md).
It is technical reference material, not a rule for choosing German-learning
sources, vocabulary, or curriculum.
